 :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5dec16f-fb09-5fd8-a373-8b5530c8c3d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467394} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3c67884-b3b3-57af-9b57-948f0bc78e32" :answer-field/key "$-\\sqrt{4}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468418} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a08dfa2b-4663-56c3-8327-10b978449b1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467396} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c88fde-35c4-5dd4-a0c5-2aaab0eb35e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3c88fde-35c4-5dd4-a0c5-2aaab0eb35e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467397} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd49c2fb-b74c-5701-921e-2021a40da2cf" :answer-field/key "$\\frac{1}{9}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12109bd9-1ea8-5fd9-b15c-ba4373902b35" :question/math-academy-id "q-37383" :question/problem "Which of the numbers below belong to  ${Q}_{0}^{+}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that ${Q}_{0}^{+}$ is the set of all positive rational numbers and $0.$\n\n- $0$ belongs to ${Q}_{0}^{+}.\\,✓$\n\n- $\\sqrt{27}=3\\sqrt{3}$ is not a rational number, so it does *not* belong to ${Q}_{0}^{+}.\\,\\times $\n\n- $-\\sqrt{4}=-2$ is a negative number, so it does *not* belong to ${Q}_{0}^{+}.\\,\\times $\n\n- $\\frac{1}{9}$ is a rational number because it is the ratio of two integers, and moreover, it is a positive rational number. Therefore, $\\frac{1}{9}$ belongs to ${Q}_{0}^{+}.\\,✓$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is “The number $0$”; $\\frac{1}{9}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323917 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124654 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca319c5-9f62-51e3-b670-611fc8a8daaa" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Critical Point Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051048 :topic/id #uuid "7d157e41-c50b-570b-8b5b-b8a5a0d03163" :topic/math-academy-id 1944 :topic/title "The Second Partial Derivatives Test"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76120093-e343-5052-a462-9eeed4935b72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450594} {:answer/id #uuid "555c0670-0865-5a00-b027-f58a0b26c69f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "555c0670-0865-5a00-b027-f58a0b26c69f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450595} :answer-field/id #uuid "3298d537-d11e-5eb5-b471-be1eb348f6fe" :answer-field/key "$H(4,8)>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451251} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e466c1b-7f85-5787-9f33-f387b0440aa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450596} {:answer/id #uuid "3a2b7b99-7dac-506f-8f5e-60849b213bf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e466c1b-7f85-5787-9f33-f387b0440aa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450596} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c78cee0-1b49-5dcb-820f-d25ea871fcfa" :answer-field/key "$\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(4,8)>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451252} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d90c823-2374-59b3-b675-22903d76217f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450598} {:answer/id #uuid "566b5d8d-7821-54bd-ac4b-b928d840baa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d90c823-2374-59b3-b675-22903d76217f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450598} :answer-field/id #uuid "c44d6730-238d-5968-83bf-bfd50fc8649d" :answer-field/key "The point $(4,8)$ is a saddle point of $f$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14dd51d6-4da8-56bc-8036-6e67390713d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-37449" :question/problem "The function   $f(x,y)$\nhas the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y & 4x-2y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(4,8).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y & 4x-2y\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=4x-2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y)=-3{x}^{2}+4x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x-2y)=-2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-2y)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3{x}^{2}+4x & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(4,8),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(4,8)=\\begin{bmatrix}-32 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $H(4,8)>0$ is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(4,8),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(4,8)=det({f}^{″}(4,8))=(-32)\\cdot (-2)-{4}^{2}=48>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(4,8)>0$ is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(4,8)=({f}^{″}(4,8))_{11}=-32<0.$\n\n- Statement “The point $(4,8)$ is a saddle point of $f$” is false. Since $H(4,8)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(4,8)<0,$ the point $(4,8)$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $H(4,8)>0$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323919 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124528 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20a6c0cf-04d4-5908-a03f-1adf491b9282" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050470 :topic/id #uuid "4c435cda-6bf3-50f8-af96-25002a1726eb" :topic/math-academy-id 1966 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7f09b1c-9964-5ae6-9322-bf6de2b57681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467398} {:answer/id #uuid "dd858fdf-4fbe-5af0-b7f7-3d32c5fc5ff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd858fdf-4fbe-5af0-b7f7-3d32c5fc5ff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467399} :answer-field/id #uuid "e939d814-ae4b-507f-b423-19079a26ac60" :answer-field/key "${\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}+1=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468420} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2758e74-ea85-5f56-a8cf-c1056f59c3d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467400} {:answer/id #uuid "e83bbd17-0d14-5570-83ce-8018039ac54b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e83bbd17-0d14-5570-83ce-8018039ac54b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467401} :answer-field/id #uuid "6089e5b4-5b31-5cc3-8e8f-3c2b0ef217f0" :answer-field/key "$(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +5)=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468421} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3adb0444-ee54-507e-bc74-75995133eade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467402} {:answer/id #uuid "e35df19c-c12d-5884-9bab-6220a82bac4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3adb0444-ee54-507e-bc74-75995133eade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467402} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfa1370c-c573-5558-8a76-57bd2125c1c9" :answer-field/key "${\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda =0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "26a6234c-3080-57fe-a159-1f4332413072" :question/math-academy-id "q-37527" :question/problem "If $B$ is a $3\\times 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $B?$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ is invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $0$ can't be an eigenvalue of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda =0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation ${\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}+1=0$ could  be the characteristic equation of $B.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation $(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +5)=0$ could be the characteristic equation of $B.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation ${\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda =0$ **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $B$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}+1=0$; $(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +5)=0$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326671 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048146 :topic/id #uuid "832923c0-f0e3-599f-af85-37cb7dae2658" :topic/math-academy-id 153 :topic/title "The Determinant of a 3x3 Matrix"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6ffe837-0ba9-566a-847f-01c50e5b0d4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438254} {:answer/id #uuid "0c851c6e-8e6f-5ce1-85f0-794bba8508bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c851c6e-8e6f-5ce1-85f0-794bba8508bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438255} :answer-field/id #uuid "ece7019d-4e4a-54c5-8c4b-3ee796bd1176" :answer-field/key "$E$ is a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439156} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69b3cb09-83cc-54f2-9f3a-46d5234e0bc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438256} {:answer/id #uuid "e013cbff-c909-515f-a71d-027cdb4811c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69b3cb09-83cc-54f2-9f3a-46d5234e0bc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438256} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bcb1241-521a-5c92-ac65-6398206509ea" :answer-field/key "$E$ is **not**  a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439157} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1785546f-b00e-5a43-be50-0c26a6fc2ca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438258} {:answer/id #uuid "1c201000-e71f-5609-9f1d-19bba5ab4630" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1785546f-b00e-5a43-be50-0c26a6fc2ca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438258} :answer-field/id #uuid "e28dc200-2ac1-54de-90ea-6e018bd1ba08" :answer-field/key "$det(E)=-2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "084b3fe8-adf9-5ab7-9017-b2d4d829ca62" :question/math-academy-id "q-38179" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $E$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nE=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & 3 & 4 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 5 & 6 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 7 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(E) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & 3 & 4 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 5 & 6 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 7 & 8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}|\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|-3|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & 6 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 8\\end{aligned}|+4|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & 5 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(40-42)-3(\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{12}{3})+4(\\frac{14}{3}-\\frac{10}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}+4\\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{12}{3}+\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(E)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement “$E$ is a singular matrix” is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326673 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048146 :topic/id #uuid "832923c0-f0e3-599f-af85-37cb7dae2658" :topic/math-academy-id 153 :topic/title "The Determinant of a 3x3 Matrix"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79e4e142-7005-54c1-8d51-b38fccf87ef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438260} {:answer/id #uuid "54cfd4e5-c485-53e9-8b91-e2afca217595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438261}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79e4e142-7005-54c1-8d51-b38fccf87ef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438260} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc0815ad-f485-5e0c-8048-0318325f0ad4" :answer-field/key "$Q$ is **not** a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439159} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f7c0c3a-e50c-57b3-b6e3-787c897947c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438262} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0b4616-bf0b-52ad-8642-7f8e6022ec10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c0b4616-bf0b-52ad-8642-7f8e6022ec10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438263} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef01e74d-81a9-5880-81c3-faa3592706db" :answer-field/key "$Q$ is a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439160} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ce5156b-be05-52db-8fa2-508a2bebc677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438264} {:answer/id #uuid "5c652fd1-d715-5a76-8fb9-1477a177d25a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ce5156b-be05-52db-8fa2-508a2bebc677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438264} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4c6df6b-8841-5610-8b0d-3ae518f8442e" :answer-field/key "$det(Q)=-5$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2a72ef8-dad7-5172-8868-8d2781696df8" :question/math-academy-id "q-38182" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $Q$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Q) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0|\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 \\\\ 5 & 8\\end{aligned}|-3|\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}|+6|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0-3(8-14)+6(5-8) \\\\  & =0-3\\cdot (-6)+6\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =18-18 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(Q)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement “$Q$ is a singular matrix” is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326681 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81247553-750f-5bf7-a1c4-698ad63b5ae4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Left- and Right-Continuity" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050657 :topic/id #uuid "5cf444d7-d014-59c7-b47c-21b442662b58" :topic/math-academy-id 474 :topic/title "Left and Right Continuity"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65112821-7bee-5d65-9072-8d6df8efb997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467404} {:answer/id #uuid "d1d25dfc-3ea3-5c6f-a70a-c04b8cb587f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65112821-7bee-5d65-9072-8d6df8efb997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467404} :answer-field/id #uuid "08a64ff2-0f5c-592a-a694-96380d251f4f" :answer-field/key "The function is left-continuous at $x=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468423} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7b22f46-00f3-5f71-8433-0e28bd44d4fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467406} {:answer/id #uuid "a910c191-586f-52d0-bc58-2320ce2cc779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a910c191-586f-52d0-bc58-2320ce2cc779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467407} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ec8a870-a660-5ca5-87da-e92722d29f6d" :answer-field/key "The function is right-continuous at $x=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468424} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ee5ac67-1f2f-5723-8699-c4b5f9b6a498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467408} {:answer/id #uuid "4a6697d9-9225-5cba-a043-0834dae41039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ee5ac67-1f2f-5723-8699-c4b5f9b6a498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467408} :answer-field/id #uuid "108e3dce-9c13-5825-b131-e2991330ae58" :answer-field/key "The function is continuous at $x=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468425}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2437419d-408f-504c-b75e-afb16e745cc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-38355" :question/problem "![](images/71/718dd1f38f7c790ab83639f69462b372de6b7e8501c0a4d6ff678340ab4154bd.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “The function is left-continuous at $x=0$” is false.  The function is not left-continuous at $x=0,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=3\\ne 0=f(0)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is right-continuous at $x=0$” is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=0,$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=0=f(0)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is continuous at $x=0$” is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=0,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not left-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement “The function is right-continuous at $x=0$” is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326691 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81247553-750f-5bf7-a1c4-698ad63b5ae4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Left- and Right-Continuity" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050657 :topic/id #uuid "5cf444d7-d014-59c7-b47c-21b442662b58" :topic/math-academy-id 474 :topic/title "Left and Right Continuity"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "005e3900-6b8d-5e21-aabf-447866db29b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467410} {:answer/id #uuid "0a7071b6-9e18-5659-87c6-c1ca0e09122e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "005e3900-6b8d-5e21-aabf-447866db29b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467410} :answer-field/id #uuid "10ef3452-9b61-54d2-b8e0-304d34f04a95" :answer-field/key "The function is left-continuous at $x=-1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468426} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f985471b-d365-53ab-9bab-43f9b73d52e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467412} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3c3c75-c48d-56a2-960c-79fab11bfc0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f985471b-d365-53ab-9bab-43f9b73d52e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467412} :answer-field/id #uuid "645f6342-086b-5864-b40f-faf3311eb59f" :answer-field/key "The function is right-continuous at $x=-1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468427} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c2ecd50-6f40-5ddf-b6cc-27bce778c863" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467414} {:answer/id #uuid "8a1126a1-58d2-5ef3-96eb-cf4bf898dec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c2ecd50-6f40-5ddf-b6cc-27bce778c863" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467414} :answer-field/id #uuid "977f0cda-4b4d-5298-9b59-46efd06a7e03" :answer-field/key "The function is continuous at $x=-1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aa66e43c-92cc-5831-9942-3be250ef9ed8" :question/math-academy-id "q-38546" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8fdb835ed2998fae4d8afc3fd5e593bac6c72b1ee45c8b4fe01bdc4348322d1e.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “The function is left-continuous at $x=-1$” is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=-1\\ne 1=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is right-continuous at $x=-1$” is false. The function is not right-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=-1\\ne 1=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is continuous at $x=-1$” is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is neither left-continuous nor right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326693 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129148 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34aa8f4e-20f1-5dda-ac53-6eb411543897" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Polynomial" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050046 :topic/id #uuid "288ef1bc-9e03-59cc-957a-e1edeebef5c3" :topic/math-academy-id 2050 :topic/title "End Behavior of Polynomials"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07a4273a-8114-5443-8534-d3c0afff82d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467416} {:answer/id #uuid "170156c0-7b48-5db9-b7d9-e492492824c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07a4273a-8114-5443-8534-d3c0afff82d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/t