ox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a410e5f1-db4f-5d99-8a2f-cd16595bdd74" :question/math-academy-id "q-47923" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x}, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ \\,\\,1, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- Let first consider the interval  $[-1,1].$ This is a  closed interval, but we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x}=\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1]$, and the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now consider  $[0.5,1].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=0,$ which is not in the interval  $[0.5,1].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[0.5,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we note that the interval $[-1,0)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[0.5,1]$ only." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048748 :topic/id #uuid "bad1cfe2-7639-5714-8417-2b8af7ece5cb" :topic/math-academy-id 3571 :topic/title "The Extreme Value Theorem"} :activity/title "The Extreme Value Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508384} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509010}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 175921